causal-loop 1
title "Why mental health at work stays out of sight"

# Why mental health at work stays out of sight: the structure of a wicked problem.
#
# Written for this repository, from a Dutch set of notes on mental health in
# working life. The labels are in English, like the other examples beside this
# one; where the notes quote a phrase, the Dutch original is kept in a link's
# note or in a comment. No person in the notes is named here.
#
# This diagram offers no solutions. It shows only the structure: three core
# variables - experienced self-direction, emotion suppression and experienced
# meaning - the loops that run through them, the cultural norm and the outside
# constraints that press on them, and the loops that keep the whole problem
# invisible. Nothing is simulated.

# ---- variables ---------------------------------------------------------------

# The three core variables.
variable selfDirection "Experienced self-direction"
variable emotionSuppression "Emotion suppression (downplaying as coping)"
variable meaning "Experienced meaning and perspective"

# Goal orientation, the bridge between self-direction and suppression.
variable goalOrientation "Goal orientation"
variable goalsAchieved "Goals achieved"
variable satisfaction "Satisfaction"
variable bigPicture "Sight of the bigger picture"

# Meaning and sacrifice, the bridge between self-direction and the bigger picture.
variable sacrifice "Willingness to sacrifice"
variable progress "Progress"
variable confidence "Confidence"

# The short-term coping loop and where it ends.
variable shortTermFunctioning "Functioning in the short term"
variable copingConfirmation "Confirmation of the coping strategy"
variable exhaustion "Exhaustion"
variable dropout "Mental health dropout"

# The cultural norm.
variable toughNorm "Norm: don't make a fuss" start=0.8
variable emotionExpression "Expression of emotion"

# The constraints of the system, outside every loop.
variable legalFrame "Legal limits on discussing mental health" start=0.8
variable insurerDependence "Dependence on insurers and premiums" start=0.7
variable ageing "Ageing population" start=0.7

# Invisibility.
variable conversationalSafety "Safety to talk"
variable openness "Openness and vulnerability"
variable visibleComplaints "Visible mental health complaints"
variable preventionUrgency "Perceived need for prevention"
variable systemChange "System change"

# The economics of prevention.
variable preventionInvestment "Investment in prevention"
variable premiumPressure "Costs and premium pressure"

# Labels handed down.
variable generationalLabels "Limiting stories and labels of older generations"
variable youthSelfImage "Self-image of young people"
variable withdrawal "Withdrawal and behaviour"
variable vulnerability "Showing vulnerability"

# ---- short-term coping -------------------------------------------------------
# Downplaying works, for now: whoever does not feel it keeps functioning, and
# functioning confirms that downplaying works. Nothing in this path is
# negative, so it compounds.

link emotionSuppression -> shortTermFunctioning + "relativeren als coping"
link shortTermFunctioning -> copingConfirmation +
link copingConfirmation -> emotionSuppression +

loop R1 "Coping that confirms itself" emotionSuppression shortTermFunctioning copingConfirmation

# The price comes later. Keeping up an untrue story is tiring, and exhaustion
# eventually costs the very functioning that confirmed the strategy. One
# negative link: balancing, and delayed, which is why the strategy looks sound
# for so long.

link emotionSuppression -> exhaustion + delayed "keeping up an untrue story is tiring"
link exhaustion -> shortTermFunctioning - delayed
link exhaustion -> dropout + delayed "uitval"

loop B1 "An untrue story wears out" emotionSuppression exhaustion shortTermFunctioning copingConfirmation

# ---- meaning and perspective -------------------------------------------------
# A clear perspective - "waar doe ik het voor" - makes sacrifice bearable, a
# clear way out. Sacrifice buys progress, progress builds confidence and
# self-direction, and self-direction keeps the perspective in view.

link meaning -> sacrifice + "a clear way out"
link sacrifice -> progress +
link progress -> confidence +
link confidence -> selfDirection +
link selfDirection -> meaning + "back to the perspective"

loop R2 "Perspective carries the cost" meaning sacrifice progress confidence selfDirection

# ---- goal orientation --------------------------------------------------------
# Self-direction shows itself as goal orientation, and goal orientation is the
# bridge to suppression: a goal is also a place to flee to, away from what is
# felt. Goals achieved bring satisfaction, and satisfaction sharpens the focus
# on the next goal. And the closer the next target, the smaller the bigger
# picture.

link selfDirection -> goalOrientation + "the bridge to suppression"
link goalOrientation -> emotionSuppression + "flight into goals"
link goalOrientation -> goalsAchieved +
link goalsAchieved -> satisfaction +
link satisfaction -> goalOrientation +
link goalOrientation -> bigPicture - "eyes on the next target"

loop R3 "Goals feed goals" goalOrientation goalsAchieved satisfaction

# The two bridges meet. Self-direction drives goal orientation, which narrows
# the bigger picture that meaning is drawn from. One negative link: balancing.

link bigPicture -> meaning + "the bridge to the bigger picture"

loop B2 "Goals narrow the view" selfDirection goalOrientation bigPicture meaning sacrifice progress confidence

# ---- the norm ----------------------------------------------------------------
# "Niet aanstellen, niet zeiken." The norm holds back the expression of
# emotion, and it keeps complaints out of sight even where they exist. It sits
# outside every loop, and presses on them all.

link toughNorm -> emotionExpression - "niet aanstellen, niet zeiken"
link toughNorm -> visibleComplaints -
link emotionSuppression -> emotionExpression -
link emotionExpression -> visibleComplaints +

# ---- constraints of the system -----------------------------------------------
# Employers may not discuss mental health complaints; employers and employees
# depend on insurers and their premiums; the population is ageing. None of
# these is moved by anything in this diagram, and each of them lowers the
# safety to talk or the self-direction a person experiences.

link legalFrame -> conversationalSafety - "employers may not discuss complaints"
link insurerDependence -> selfDirection -
link insurerDependence -> conversationalSafety -
link ageing -> selfDirection -

# ---- invisibility ------------------------------------------------------------
# Where it is not safe to talk, nobody opens up; complaints nobody voices are
# not seen; what is not seen creates no need for prevention; and without that
# need the system does not change, so talking stays unsafe. No negative link:
# reinforcing, in whichever direction it runs.

link conversationalSafety -> openness +
link openness -> visibleComplaints +
link visibleComplaints -> preventionUrgency +
link preventionUrgency -> systemChange +
link systemChange -> conversationalSafety + delayed

loop R4 "What is not seen does not change" conversationalSafety openness visibleComplaints preventionUrgency systemChange

# ---- the economics of prevention ---------------------------------------------
# Investing in prevention means less dropout, less dropout means lower costs
# and premium pressure, and lower pressure leaves room to invest. Two negative
# links: reinforcing, as a virtuous circle or a vicious one.

link preventionUrgency -> preventionInvestment +
link preventionInvestment -> dropout - delayed
link dropout -> premiumPressure +
link premiumPressure -> preventionInvestment - "no room left for prevention"

loop R5 "Prevention pays for prevention" preventionInvestment dropout premiumPressure

# ---- labels handed down ------------------------------------------------------
# Limiting stories and labels from older generations shape how young people
# see themselves; a poorer self-image shows as withdrawal; and withdrawal reads
# as proof that the label was right. Two negative links: reinforcing.
#
# Showing vulnerability works against the labels. It is a counterforce in the
# structure, not a remedy this diagram proposes.

link generationalLabels -> youthSelfImage -
link youthSelfImage -> withdrawal -
link withdrawal -> generationalLabels + "the label confirmed"
link openness -> vulnerability +
link vulnerability -> generationalLabels - "kwetsbaarheid inzetten"

loop R6 "Labels that confirm themselves" generationalLabels youthSelfImage withdrawal
